WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.470
<v Instructor>Okay, so there are more tools</v>

2
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.210
to manage your costs in AWS.

3
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.460
The first one is AWS Budgets,

4
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.690
which allows you create a budget,

5
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.010
and then send alarms,

6
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.560
when the costs are exceeding your budgets.

7
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.730
So you have four kinds of budget

8
00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.890
you can define in the Budget service.

9
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.700
You have usage, cost, reservation, and savings plan.

10
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.030
And so you define what you want for which type of budget,

11
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.800
and then you will receive an alarm.

12
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.500
So in the case of reserved instances,

13
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.510
this budget, you can track the utilization

14
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.920
of these ROIs.

15
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.120
It supports EC2, ElastiCache, RDS, and Redshift

16
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.410
And you can define up to five SNS notifications per budget.

17
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.386
Now these budgets are very granular.

18
00:00:39.386 --> 00:00:42.390
You can filter by service, linked account, tag,

19
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.791
purchase option, instance type, region,

20
00:00:44.791 --> 00:00:45.624
region availability zone,

21
00:00:45.624 --> 00:00:47.190
API operation, et cetera, et cetera,

22
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.970
which are the same options as Cost Explorer.

23
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.110
The first two budgets are free,

24
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.870
but then you're going to pay two cents per day per budget.

25
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.300
We also have Budget Actions.

26
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.940
So the idea is that you want the budget service

27
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.920
to run an action on your behalf,

28
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.380
whenever a budget exceeds a certain cost,

29
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.180
or usage threshold.

30
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.950
We have three action types:

31
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.130
We can, for example, apply a specific IAM policy

32
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.890
to a user, a group, or an IAM role.

33
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.160
Thus, for example, restricting users, groups, or roles,

34
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.720
from doing anything.

35
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.840
We can also apply service control policies directly

36
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:22.980
into an OU,

37
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.920
again, to restrict what an account can do

38
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.210
or you can automatically stop EC2, or RDS instances.

39
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.370
These actions can be executed automatically,

40
00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.760
or they can require a workflow approval process,

41
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.310
to make sure that some human says "yes,"

42
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.890
before these actions are implemented.

43
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.690
The goal of budget actions

44
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.150
is to reduce the unintentional overspending

45
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.560
in your accounts.

46
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.440
So if we like an example of an OU,

47
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.730
with management account, and a dev OU of member accounts,

48
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.650
then we can create a budget,

49
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.250
and then in case the budget reaches a specific threshold,

50
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.070
then we can automatically apply an SCP

51
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.990
onto the development OU,

52
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.330
to make sure to restrict the member accounts.

53
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.270
So in this example, we would make the budget tailored

54
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.283
for the dev OU itself.

55
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.970
So we have two types of architecture

56
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.700
when managing budgets in AWS:

57
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.590
You have the centralized budget management,

58
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.080
and decentralized budget managements.

59
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.080
So in the centralized management of your budget,

60
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.320
here's an example, where we have the management accounts,

61
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.950
and then an OU with a member account, and a restrictive OU.

62
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.813
So we create a budget in the management accounts,

63
00:02:34.813 --> 00:02:38.790
for every member accounts that we want to monitor.

64
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.470
So if we have 10 member accounts,

65
00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.285
we create 10 budgets in the management accounts,

66
00:02:43.285 --> 00:02:46.800
but we apply a filter on each budget,

67
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.774
and the filter can be an account filter,

68
00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:53.580
so that the budget is tailored to one specific account.

69
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.170
Therefore, effectively,

70
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.170
by creating a budget into the management account,

71
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.020
we are effectively, but not actually,

72
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.563
creating a budget for the member account.

73
00:03:03.563 --> 00:03:07.500
Now, when the budget exceeds a threshold,

74
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.990
then we can send a notification into SNS,

75
00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.940
which can trigger a Lambda function,

76
00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.320
and that Lambda function

77
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.610
can have the API call embedded into its code,

78
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.990
to move the account that has breached its threshold,

79
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.390
into a more restrictive OU,

80
00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.990
where an actual SCP has already been applied.

81
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.450
So effectively, we move that member account

82
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.880
from OU to a restrictive OU,

83
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.710
and there we don't allow it

84
00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.900
to create new resources, and so on.

85
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.910
On top of it, to be notified of this move

86
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.160
we can also make the Lambda function

87
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.220
send an email to an Amazon SNS topic,

88
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.990
through an Amazon SNS topic,

89
00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.438
to our administrators, for example.

90
00:03:48.438 --> 00:03:50.744
So that is centralized budget management,

91
00:03:50.744 --> 00:03:51.990
because here,

92
00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.530
we manage all the budgets of our organization,

93
00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.843
within the management accounts itself.

94
00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.230
We also have decentralized the budget managements.

95
00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.190
So in this case,

96
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.320
while the budgets are going to be managed

97
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.780
directly into the member accounts,

98
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.750
but to simplify distributing these budgets,

99
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.700
and creating them in each member account,

100
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.070
we can use a CloudFormation stack sets,

101
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.690
to deploy these budgets.

102
00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.480
So we have a budget in each member account,

103
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:19.920
and that budget can have the same thing.

104
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.370
For example, send messages into an SNS topic,

105
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.200
and then trigger an email,

106
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.610
in case we are about to reach

107
00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.497
a specific threshold for budget.

108
00:04:28.497 --> 00:04:31.290
Or if it's a critical notification, for example,

109
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:34.740
budget has been exceeded, then again, send a notification,

110
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.260
and trigger a Lambda function that would, for example,

111
00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.060
stop all EC2 instances.

112
00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.590
And that's just one example.

113
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.850
Talking about Cost Explorer,

114
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.950
so it's a way for you to visualize, understand,

115
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.010
and manage your AWS cost and usage over time,

116
00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.300
and you create custom reports

117
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.060
that will analyze your costs, and usage data.

118
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.010
You're Going to analyze your data at a high level.

119
00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.410
So you're going to get overview into your total cost,

120
00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.150
as well as usage across all accounts.

121
00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:58.620
But you can go deeper,

122
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.690
and you can analyze it monthly, hourly,

123
00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.610
and at the resource level.

124
00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.050
Cost Explorer is a great service

125
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.630
to allow you to \choose an optimal savings plan.

126
00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.070
As we saw before, savings plans are a way

127
00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.620
to commit to a certain dollar amount over time,

128
00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.590
and lower your prices on your bill.

129
00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.252
Now with Cost Explorer, there's also a nifty feature,

130
00:05:16.252 --> 00:05:19.800
in which you can forecast usage of your cost,

131
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.550
up to 12 months in advance, based on previous usage.

132
00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.490
So if we look at Cost Explorer, this is the interface,

133
00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.800
and we get access to a lot of filters, as I said,

134
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.650
the same filters for Cost Explorer and for Budgets,

135
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.780
as well as some detailed graphs

136
00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.010
around how your costs are spread out over time, and so on.

137
00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.150
So that's it for this lecture.

138
00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.150
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

